 start with the move queen to a6  but this does not solve  white's issues and in fact this is  actually losing  for whites because black can play the  move bishop  here to b4 now we'll look at two  variations they could play you know  knight two  c3 they could also play bishop here to  d2 we'll look at both of those options  uh they both lead to white's demise here  but we'll first look at knight 2  c3 well doesn't matter we're going to  play knight 2  c5 attacking the queen queen comes back  to b5  and then just to move pawn to a6 now  white has no moves they are going to  lose their queen so let's say queen  takes here on b4  knight takes here on b4 and black should  have an easy game from here and also  black still has more threats knight to  c2 check forking the rook here on  a one now slight variation in if they  play  bishop to d2 uh you do need to change it  up a little bit after you played knight  two  c5 and they play queen two b5 uh you  need to go ahead and take first  so after they take with their knights  here you can just play pawn  to a6 and now their queen is going to  fall and the reason for that is  if you don't if you just play pawn to  a6 first well now they can take with  their queen here on  b4 take our bishop and yes we do take  their queen  but then they take with their bishop and  you can see black has given up  three minor pieces and a pawn  for the queen compared to if you just  look at  normal when they played knight to c3  here and we played knight to c5  and then all of a sudden we play a6 and  we take their  queen you can see we've only given up  two minor pieces in a pawn for the queen  so very different  uh much better situation so just make  sure if they play their bishop you do  take  the bishop first check so they have to  respond to it and then you play that  pawn to  a6 okay so we've seen from white  standpoint that queen to a6  is not going to go well for them so what  other options do they have one of them  is going to be  bis